Falsifies `paging::sv39::the_user_va_gate_admits_only_the_aligned_low_half`.

The defect: `is_user_page_va` asks whether the address is *not in the kernel half*
instead of whether it is in the user half. Those read as the same question and are
not, because the two halves are not exhaustive: on Sv39 an address is canonical
only if bits 63:38 are all copies of bit 38, and a VA in neither half is a hole.
With this, a user program may name a non-canonical address and the walk indexes on
bits that mean nothing, which is a translation the hardware and the kernel disagree
about.

Expected to fail: `assert_eq!(is_user_page_va::<Sv39>(va), va & 0xfff == 0 && va >>
38 == 0)`, on any aligned non-canonical address. The second assertion,
`assert!(Sv39::is_in_half(Half::Low, va) && !Sv39::is_in_half(Half::High, va))`, fails
with it for the same address, and that is worth having: it is the one that says
"in the user half" out loud rather than by arithmetic.

**The gate is shared and the proof is not.** `is_user_page_va` is one generic
function in `lib.rs`, and DECISIONS §19 has each ISA state the property itself, so
this one function has three independent proofs over it. The `aarch64` and `x86_64`
records break it in two other ways; the three patches are not copies.

diff --git a/crates/paging/src/page_format.rs b/crates/paging/src/page_format.rs
--- a/crates/paging/src/page_format.rs
+++ b/crates/paging/src/page_format.rs
@@ -40,7 +40,7 @@
 /// assert_eq!(PAGE_SIZE, 4096);
 /// ```
 pub fn is_user_page_va<F: PageFormat>(va: u64) -> bool {
-    F::is_in_half(Half::Low, va) && va.is_multiple_of(PAGE_SIZE)
+    !F::is_in_half(Half::High, va) && va.is_multiple_of(PAGE_SIZE)
 }
 
 /// **The seam between the shared walk and a hardware page-table format.**
